class=3D"anchor" aria-label=3D"Permalink: Q. What does the cascading portio=
n of CSS mean?" href=3D"https://github.com/learning-zone/css-basics#q-what-=
does-the-cascading-portion-of-css-mean"><svg data-component=3D"Octicon" cla=
ss=3D"octicon octicon-link" viewBox=3D"0 0 16 16" version=3D"1.1" width=3D"=
16" height=3D"16" aria-hidden=3D"true"><path d=3D"m7.775 3.275 1.25-1.25a3.=
5 3.5 0 1 1 4.95 4.95l-2.5 2.5a3.5 3.5 0 0 1-4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1 .018-1.0=
42.751.751 0 0 1 1.042-.018 1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l2.5-2.5a2.002 2.002 0 =
0 0-2.83-2.83l-1.25 1.25a.751.751 0 0 1-1.042-.018.751.751 0 0 1-.018-1.042=
Zm-4.69 9.64a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 2.83 0l1.25-1.25a.751.751 0 0 1 1.042.018.75=
1.751 0 0 1 .018 1.042l-1.25 1.25a3.5 3.5 0 1 1-4.95-4.95l2.5-2.5a3.5 3.5 0=
 0 1 4.95 0 .751.751 0 0 1-.018 1.042.751.751 0 0 1-1.042.018 1.998 1.998 0=
 0 0-2.83 0l-2.5 2.5a1.998 1.998 0 0 0 0 2.83Z"></path></svg></a></div>
<p dir=3D"auto">The <strong>cascading</strong> in CSS refers to the fact th=
at styling rules "cascade" down from several sources. This means that CSS h=
as an inherent hierarchy and styles of a higher precedence will overwrite r=
ules of a lower precedence.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Even the simplest HTML document may have three or more styl=
e sheets associated with it including:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>The browser's style sheet</li>
<li>The user's style sheet</li>
<li>The author's style sheet</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>1. Browser style sheets:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Browsers apply style sheets to all web documents. Although =
these style sheets vary from browser to browser, they all have common chara=
cteristics such as black text, blue links, purple visited links etc. These =
are referred to as a "default" browser stylesheet.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">As soon as you, the author, apply a style sheet to a docume=
nt, it will override the browser's style sheet. This is because author styl=
e sheets will always override browser style sheets.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>2. User style sheets:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Most modern browsers allow users to set their own style she=
ets within their browser. These style sheets will override the browsers def=
ault style sheets - for that user only.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>3. Author style sheets:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">As soon as you apply a basic style sheet or an inline style=
 to a page, you have added what is referred to as an "author style sheet". =
Everything you do, from choosing fonts, colours and laying out pages in CSS=
 is done using author style sheets.</p>
<div align=3D"right" dir=3D"auto">
    <b><a href=3D"https://github.com/learning-zone/css-basics#">=E2=86=A5 b=
ack to top</a></b>
</div>
<div class=3D"markdown-heading" dir=3D"auto"><h2 tabindex=3D"-1" class=3D"h=
eading-element" dir=3D"auto">Q. How does CSS work under the hood?</h2><a id=
=3D"user-content-q-how-does-css-work-under-the-hood" class=3D"anchor" aria-=
label=3D"Permalink: Q. How does CSS work under the hood?" href=3D"https://g=
