<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Mobile-first design</strong> means writing base CSS=
 for the smallest screen first and then progressively enhancing the layout =
for larger screens using <code>min-width</code> media queries.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Why it is recommended:</strong></p>
<ol dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Performance</strong>: Mobile devices receive only the base styl=
es; larger screens load additional styles on top. Unused CSS is minimised f=
or the most bandwidth-constrained devices.</li>
<li><strong>Progressive enhancement</strong>: Forces you to prioritise core=
 content and functionality before adding enhancements.</li>
<li><strong>Simpler CSS</strong>: Overriding simple mobile styles upward is=
 usually less complex than overriding complex desktop styles downward.</li>
<li><strong>SEO &amp; Google</strong>: Google uses mobile-first indexing, s=
o mobile-friendly pages rank better.</li>
</ol>
