<p dir=3D"auto">CSS gradients are represented by the <code>&lt;gradient&gt;=
</code> data type, a special type of <code>&lt;image&gt;</code> made of a p=
rogressive transition between two or more colors. There are three types of =
gradients:</p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li>linear (created with the <code>linear-gradient()</code> function),</li>
<li>radial (created with <code>radial-gradient()</code>), and</li>
<li>conic (created with the <code>conic-gradient()</code> function).</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto">We can also create repeating gradients with the <code>repea=
ting-linear-gradient()</code>, <code>repeating-radial-gradient()</code>, an=
d <code>repeating-conic-gradient()</code> functions.</p>
