<tbody>
<tr>
<td>01.</td>
<td>inline</td>
<td>Displays an element as an inline element (like <code>&lt;span&gt;</code=
>). Any height and width properties will have no effect</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>02.</td>
<td>block</td>
<td>Displays an element as a block element (like <code>&lt;p&gt;</code>). I=
t starts on a new line, and takes up the whole width</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>03.</td>
<td>contents</td>
<td>Makes the container disappear, making the child elements children of th=
e element the next level up in the DOM</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>04.</td>
<td>flex</td>
<td>Displays an element as a block-level flex container</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>05.</td>
<td>grid</td>
<td>Displays an element as a block-level grid container</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>06.</td>
<td>inline-block</td>
<td>Displays an element as an inline-level block container. The element its=
elf is formatted as an inline element, but you can apply height and width v=
alues</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>07.</td>
<td>inline-flex</td>
<td>Displays an element as an inline-level flex container</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>08.</td>
<td>inline-grid</td>
<td>Displays an element as an inline-level grid container</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>09.</td>
<td>inline-table</td>
