<p dir=3D"auto">In order to have crisp, good-looking graphics that make the=
 best of retina displays we need to use high resolution images whenever pos=
sible. However using always the highest resolution images will have an impa=
ct on performance as more bytes will need to be sent over the wire.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">To overcome this problem, we can use responsive images, as =
specified in HTML5. It requires making available different resolution files=
 of the same image to the browser and let it decide which image is best, us=
ing the html attribute <code>srcset</code> and optionally <code>sizes</code=
>, for instance:</p>
<div class=3D"highlight highlight-text-html-basic notranslate position-rela=
tive overflow-auto" dir=3D"auto"><pre><span class=3D"pl-kos">&lt;</span><sp=
an class=3D"pl-ent">div</span> <span class=3D"pl-c1">responsive-background-=
image</span><span class=3D"pl-kos">&gt;</span> =20
  <span class=3D"pl-kos">&lt;</span><span class=3D"pl-ent">img</span> <span=
 class=3D"pl-c1">src</span>=3D"<span class=3D"pl-s">/images/foo.png</span>"=
 <span class=3D"pl-c1">alt</span>=3D"<span class=3D"pl-s">bar</span>" <span=
 class=3D"pl-c1">srcset</span>=3D"<span class=3D"pl-s">/images/foo.png 2x</=
span>" <span class=3D"pl-kos">/&gt;</span>
<span class=3D"pl-kos">&lt;/</span><span class=3D"pl-ent">div</span><span c=
lass=3D"pl-kos">&gt;</span>

<span class=3D"pl-c">&lt;!-- It enable the browser to use a medium and larg=
e image based on </span>
<span class=3D"pl-c">     the sizes of the image in the viewport</span>
<span class=3D"pl-c">--&gt;</span>
<span class=3D"pl-kos">&lt;</span><span class=3D"pl-ent">img</span>
  <span class=3D"pl-c1">src</span>=3D"<span class=3D"pl-s">/images/foo.png<=
/span>"
  <span class=3D"pl-c1">alt</span>=3D"<span class=3D"pl-s">bar</span>"
  <span class=3D"pl-c1">srcset</span>=3D"<span class=3D"pl-s">/images/foo-m=
