<p dir=3D"auto">Progressive rendering is the name given to techniques used =
to improve the performance of a webpage (in particular, improve perceived l=
oad time) to render content for display as quickly as possible.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Examples:</strong></p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Lazy loading of images</strong> - Images on the page are not lo=
aded all at once. JavaScript will be used to load an image when the user sc=
rolls into the part of the page that displays the image.</li>
<li><strong>Prioritizing visible content (or above-the-fold rendering)</str=
ong> - Include only the minimum CSS/content/scripts necessary for the amoun=
t of page that would be rendered in the users browser first to display as q=
uickly as possible, you can then use deferred scripts or listen for the DOM=
ContentLoaded/load event to load in other resources and content.</li>
<li><strong>Async HTML fragments</strong> - Flushing parts of the HTML to t=
he browser as the page is constructed on the back end.</li>
</ul>
